From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matthew Cather <mattbob4@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 14:00:26 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] libsanitizer: shrink the HWASan primary allocator on aarch64

The HWASan primary allocator reserves a fixed 4T address space
region on Linux. An aarch64 kernel built with a 39 bit virtual
address space, the OpenWrt default for 4K pages, gives user space
only 512G, so the reservation fails and every instrumented process
aborts at startup in mmap.

Android meets the same limit on its 39, 42 and 48 bit configurations
and uses a 128G allocator with VeryDenseSizeClassMap. Exclude
aarch64 from the 4T Linux branch so it falls through to that 128G
configuration, which fits every aarch64 VA size.

Signed-off-by: Matthew Cather <mattbob4@gmail.com>
---
 libsanitizer/hwasan/hwasan_allocator.h |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/libsanitizer/hwasan/hwasan_allocator.h
+++ b/libsanitizer/hwasan/hwasan_allocator.h
@@ -74,7 +74,7 @@ struct AP64 {
 #if defined(HWASAN_ALIASING_MODE)
   static const uptr kSpaceSize = 1ULL << kAddressTagShift;
   typedef __sanitizer::DefaultSizeClassMap SizeClassMap;
-#elif SANITIZER_LINUX && !SANITIZER_ANDROID
+#elif SANITIZER_LINUX && !SANITIZER_ANDROID && !defined(__aarch64__)
   static const uptr kSpaceSize = 0x40000000000ULL;  // 4T.
   typedef __sanitizer::DefaultSizeClassMap SizeClassMap;
 #else
